    <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Foundation</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sumanguru-kante"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sumanguru-kante.jpg" alt="Sumanguru Kanté" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sumanguru-kante">Sumanguru Kanté</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ghana_successor_map_1200.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">T L Miles (talk), CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mali Empire was founded in the early 13th century, emerging from the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> and the fragmentation of smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mandinka">Mandinka</a> chiefdoms. Its establishment is attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a>, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#keita-dynasty">Keita clan</a> who, around 1235, forged a coalition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mema">Mema</a>, Wagadou and Mandinka forces to confront <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sumanguru-kante">Sumanguru Kanté</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sosso-empire">Sosso</a> kingdom at the decisive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a>. Victory enabled Sundiata to unite the Manding chiefdoms under his authority, inaugurating the Mali Empire, whose name is traditionally interpreted as 'where the king lives'. Tradition credits Sundiata with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, an assembly and charter regulating succession, clan obligations and social order, which laid the institutional foundations for a state that would dominate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>. The subjugation of the old Ghanaian heartland, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali-conquest-of-koumbi-saleh">capture of Koumbi Saleh</a>, its former capital, traditionally dated to 1240, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a> access to critical trade routes and goldfields, setting the stage for both territorial expansion and the accumulation of wealth. After Sundiata's death around 1255, his successors, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-uli">Mansa Uli</a>, expanded the empire towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#senegal-river">Senegal River</a> and opened ties with the wider Islamic world, exemplified by Uli's pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timbuktu.jpg" alt="Timbuktu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Donkeys,_Timbuktu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flickr user: Emilio Labrador Santiago de Chile https://www.flickr.com/people/3059349393/, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s economy was anchored in control of the gold-producing regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bambuk">Bambuk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bure">Bure</a>, complemented by the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#taghaza">Taghaza</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahara">Sahara</a> and the trans-Saharan trade networks that linked them. Society combined a hereditary aristocracy led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#keita-dynasty">Keita dynasty</a> with subordinate local chiefs, Muslim scholars and hereditary occupational groups such as smiths and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#griots">griots</a> (jeli), while most of the population engaged in farming, herding, fishing and craft production. Cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gao">Gao</a> became commercial and intellectual hubs, fostering trade, Islamic scholarship and the arts. Revenue came from tribute paid by subject territories and from duties on trade, particularly the gold and salt caravans crossing the Sahara, while subjects also owed labour obligations to the state. By the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> (c. 1312–1337), Mali had become internationally renowned for its wealth, sophistication and cosmopolitanism. Its population included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mandinka">Mandinka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#soninke">Soninke</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fula">Fulani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuareg">Tuareg</a> communities, fostering cultural exchange and binding peripheral regions into a coherent imperial economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Military</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/songhai-empire.jpg" alt="Songhai Empire" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Songhay_Empire.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HetmanTheResearcher, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s military strength underpinned its territorial dominance, combining a core of royal troops, levies from subject communities and an elite cavalry drawn from the nobility. Early campaigns under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> emphasised coalition warfare, as seen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kirina">Kirina</a> and in the subjugation of the former Ghanaian lands. During the late 13th-century revival under the usurper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-sakura">Mansa Sakura</a>, Mali's armies extended its reach towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#niger-bend">Niger bend</a> and west towards the Atlantic, securing control of key trade arteries. The army was organised into northern and southern commands under senior generals, and the Arab writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#al-umari">al-Umari</a> reported a force of around 100,000 men, including some 10,000 cavalry, though such figures are uncertain. Military power also served as a political instrument, enforcing tribute and integrating new territories, while control of trade routes reinforced the empire's fiscal base. Campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuareg">Tuareg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and defence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mossi">Mossi</a> raids, which reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> in the 14th century, illustrate both the reach and the limits of Malian power across its vast and heterogeneous domains.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#kouroukan-fouga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga.jpg" alt="Kouroukan Fouga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%DF%9E%DF%99%DF%8E%DF%AC%DF%9E%DF%8A%DF%B2%DF%AC%DF%9D%DF%8E%DF%A5%DF%8A%DF%AB_%DF%98%DF%8F%DF%B2%DF%AC%DF%98%DF%8A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lancine.kounfantoh.fofana, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Mali Empire was governed through a strong monarchy that delegated authority to provincial governors (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#farba">farbas</a>) and local chiefs, balancing imperial oversight with local autonomy. Many conquered rulers retained their thrones as tributaries, supervised by royal representatives. Law was hybrid, combining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mandinka">Mandinka</a> customary law, traditionally traced to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islamic-jurisprudence">Islamic jurisprudence</a> administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#qadi">qadis</a>, particularly as the empire deepened its ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>. The administration included tax collectors, military officers, royal slaves serving at court and envoys responsible for maintaining cohesion across diverse regions. Succession crises, notably after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> and in the late 13th century, exposed vulnerabilities in dynastic rule, though the state proved able to recover. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>'s reign, followed by that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa">Mansa</a> Sulayman (c. 1341–1360), whose court was described by the traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> in 1352–1353, exemplified effective administration, combining religious legitimacy, diplomacy and careful oversight of trade. Power rested on royal prestige, tribute and the distribution of resources, ensuring loyalty across vast and culturally diverse territories.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#djenne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="Djenné" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moschee_von_Djenn%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">qiv, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s influence extended across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a> and into the wider Islamic world. Through control of the gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#salt-trade">salt trade</a>, the empire sustained trans-Saharan commerce linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sahel">Sahel</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#north-africa">North Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a> became centres of learning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscript</a> production, attracting scholars, merchants and pilgrims, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> is credited with commissioning the Djinguereber mosque in Timbuktu, traditionally attributed to the Andalusi poet-architect Abu Ishaq al-Sahili. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa-musas-pilgrimage">Mansa Musa's pilgrimage</a> of 1324 projected Mali's prestige abroad: his lavish spending of gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a> was remembered for years afterwards, and Mali soon appeared on European maps such as the Catalan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> of 1375. Regionally, Mali shaped political dynamics through alliances, tributary arrangements and military interventions affecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mossi">Mossi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire">Songhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuareg">Tuareg</a>. Its wealth and model of government influenced later West African states, above all Songhai, and its legacy endures in Sudano-Sahelian architecture, the epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> preserved by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#griots">griots</a>, and Islamic scholarship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block">
        <p class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</p>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#walata"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walata.jpg" alt="Walata" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#walata">Walata</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oualata_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">c.hug, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mali">Mali</a>'s decline was gradual, extending from the late 14th century into the early 17th century and driven by both internal and external pressures. Succession disputes weakened central authority, while provincial governors increasingly treated their territories as autonomous domains. External pressures included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mossi">Mossi</a> raids, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuareg-seizure-of-timbuktu">Tuareg seizure of Timbuktu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#walata">Walata</a> around 1433, and the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timbuktu">Timbuktu</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#songhai-empire">Songhai</a> ruler Sonni Ali in 1468. The shift of trade towards Songhai-controlled routes, combined with the arrival of Portuguese traders on the Atlantic coast, undermined the empire's economic foundations. By the 16th century Mali was reduced to its Manding heartland around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kangaba">Kangaba</a> and some western provinces, with diminished territory and prestige. After a failed attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#djenne">Djenné</a> in 1599, the last powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mansa">mansa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mahmud-iv">Mahmud IV</a>, died around 1610, and the kingdom split among his heirs, marking the effective end of the Mali Empire as a coherent political entity.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary"><em>Despite centuries of fragmentation, the Mali Empire's historical significance endures as a model of wealth, governance and cultural synthesis in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-africa">West Africa</a>.</em></p>

